def ACI():
    arb = show(
        "For arbitrary widths $u$ and arbitrary row counts, interchanging $(p,h,g,d,z)\\mapsto(p,d,g,h,z)$ costs $O(Vu^{\\tau})$: peel the top $\\rho$ digits to supply $W^k$ rows, pad rows to a multiple of $W^k$, cover the remaining width by base-$m$ pieces, and pad binary widths to radix $q$.",
        "Reduce a general chunk width to the power-width case by supplying the missing rows from a few high digits of the chunks themselves, then decompose the remaining width in base $m$ and sum a geometric series.",
        "The power-width procedure needs a row field divisible by $W^k$ and widths $m^k$; rows can be borrowed from the chunks' own top digits because moving them costs only $O(V\\log e)$, far less than $Ve^\\tau$.",
        "explicit",
        mq=r"Rows for a general array can be supplied by a few of its own high digits.",
        children=[
            infer("For positive $P,G,B$ and $u\\ge1$ the permutation $(p,h,g,d,z)\\mapsto(p,d,g,h,z)$ on $[P]\\times[2^u]\\times[G]\\times[2^u]\\times[B]$ costs $O(Vu^\\tau)$ steps for a fixed $0<\\tau<1$.",
                  r"in $O(Vu^\tau)$ steps, where $V=PG B\,2^{2u}$."),
            infer("Take $\\rho=\\lceil k\\log W/(2\\log q)\\rceil$ top digits with $k=\\lceil\\log_me\\rceil$, so the row field has range $q^{2\\rho}\\ge W^k$ and $\\rho=O(\\log(2e))$.",
                  r"\rho=\left\lceil\frac{k\log W}{2\log q}\right\rceil."),
            infer("Exchanging the top digits and joining them gives the field order $(h^+,h^-,g,d^+,d^-)\\to(d^+,h^+,h^-,g,d^-)\\to(d^+,h^+,d^-,g,h^-)\\to(d^+,d^-,g,h^+,h^-)$, costing $O(V\\rho)$ for the first move.",
                  r"&(h^+,h^-,g,d^+,d^-),"),
            infer("Zero rows are appended until the row count is $R'=W^k\\lceil R/W^k\\rceil<2R$, so the padded volume is less than $2V$.",
                  r"R'=W^k\left\lceil R/W^k\right\rceil<2R."),
            infer("The remaining width is written in base $m$ as $e-\\rho=\\sum_{j=0}^k a_jm^j$ with $0\\le a_j<m$, and the chunks are partitioned into $a_j$ consecutive pieces of width $m^j$.",
                  r"e-\rho=\sum_{j=0}^{k} a_jm^j,\qquad 0\le a_j<m."),
            infer("Applying the power-width interchange to each piece pair costs $O(V)\\sum_ja_jm^{j\\tau}=O(Ve^\\tau)$ since $a_j<m$ and $m^k<me$.",
                  r"O(V)\sum_j a_jm^{j\tau}=O(Ve^\tau)."),
            infer("For binary chunks take the least $e$ with $q^e\\ge2^u$ and pad each range from $[2^u]$ to $[q^e]$, at most a fixed factor $q^2$ in volume; then $e=O(u)$ and the cost is $O(Vu^\\tau)$.",
                  r"Finally, for binary chunks let $e$ be the least integer with"),
            infer("Widths differing by one bit use one extra single-bit move, costing $O(V)$.",
                  r"For widths differing by one bit, choose the leading bit of the longer"),
        ])
    power = show(
        "For $e=m^k$ and a row field divisible by $W^k$, the shear $H\\leftarrow H+D$ on two chunks of $e$ digits is carried out by running the bit network on $W$ cyclically dealt role streams, with each edge $\\Phi_{A_e}$ implemented by $\\operatorname{rank}A_e$ smaller interchanges, and the interchange of the chunks is the three shears $D\\leftarrow D-H$, $H\\leftarrow H+D$, $D\\leftarrow H-D$.",
        "Deal rows to the $W$ roles, apply the frame $\\Phi_M$ with each edge changing it, run the pointwise gates, merge rows back to undo the role permutation, and count the recursive calls.",
        "The common-frame identity says the stored array on each role ends as $\\Phi_{M_{\\rm out}-M_{\\rm in}}=\\Phi_{I_m}$ applied to the input, which is exactly the shear; each edge change is a shear of its own rank and so a bounded number of smaller interchanges.",
        "recoverable",
        children=[
            infer("The circuit data satisfy $M_{\\mathrm{out}(\\rho(w))}-M_{\\mathrm{in}(w)}=I_m$ for every wire $w$ and $s<Wm$; the bit network supplies these with $W=W_{\\rm b}$ and $s=s_{\\rm b}$.",
                  r"\quad(1\le w\le W),\qquad s<Wm."),
            infer("Rows are split cyclically into $W$ role streams within each prefix, each stream having the same remaining shape, volume $V/W$, and row count divisible by $W^{k-1}$.",
                  r"Split whole rows cyclically into $W$ role streams within each prefix"),
            infer("On every circuit edge the array is changed by $\\Phi_{A_e}$, implemented recursively by Lemma on matrix shears; each gate is executed pointwise at matching addresses.",
                  r"$\Phi_{A_e}$ to that role stream; implement it by"),
            infer("The physical output is $f'_{\\rho(w)}=\\Phi_{M_{\\rm out}}\\Phi_{-M_{\\rm in}}f_w=\\Phi_{I_m}f_w$, the same shear on every role, scratch roles included.",
                  r"=\Phi_{I_m}f_w,"),
            infer("Merging returns row $g$ of output role $\\rho(w)$ to original row $Wg+(w-1)$, undoing the role permutation and giving $H\\leftarrow H+D$ on every original row.",
                  r"Merge by returning row $g$ of"),
            infer("The full interchange is $D\\leftarrow D-H$, then $H\\leftarrow H+D$ through the network, then $D\\leftarrow H-D$; the outer two have linear cost.",
                  r"D\leftarrow D-H,\qquad H\leftarrow H+D,\qquad D\leftarrow H-D,"),
            infer("The number of recursive calls is the sum of the edge ranks, exactly $s$.",
                  r"The number of recursive calls is the sum of the edge ranks, exactly $s$."),
            infer("By the recurrence solved in the rank-saving step, $F_k=O(e^\\tau)$.",
                  r"Hence $F_k=O(e^\tau)$."),
            infer("A fixed-tape depth-first schedule parks the $W-1$ inactive role streams on a stack before each call, so cleanup stays linear in the current volume.",
                  r"Before a recursive call, append the $W-1$ inactive role streams to the"),
            infer("Each push, pop, copy, positioning or erasure moves $O(V)$ symbols at a parent node and there are only $s$ child calls, giving the additive $O(V)$ term.",
                  r"Each push, pop, copy, positioning operation, or erasure moves $O(V)$"),
        ])
    return show(
        "Two disjoint contiguous address fields of $u$ bits in an array with a complete Cartesian address set can be interchanged in $O(Vu^{\\tau})$ time for a fixed $\\tau<1$, although the intermediate steps are XOR combinations of data.",
        "Reduce the interchange to three shears, perform the middle one by running the bit network on role streams so that each edge change is a rank-$a$ shear done by $a$ smaller interchanges, apply the recurrence, and remove the width and row restrictions.",
        "Because the sum of the ranks of the edge changes is less than the number of wires times $m$, the resulting recurrence saves a power of the chunk width.",
        "explicit",
        mq=r"$m$, the resulting recurrence saves a power of the chunk width.",
        page="address-chunk-interchange",
        frm=["rank-saving-recurrence", "address-shear", "common-frame-principle", "linear-network", "subset-intersection-labels", "tape-address-model", "elementary-stream-ops", "controlled-shift"],
        salient="The final effect is an exact permutation of the data, yet the intermediate streams hold XOR combinations of different entries, and it works for arbitrary initial scratch contents.",
        adds=[("u", "chunk width", "The number of address bits in each of the two chunks to be interchanged.", "count"),
              ("k", "recursion depth", "The integer with $e=m^k$, the number of levels of the recursion.", "count")],
        children=[power, arb])


def PFK():
    return show(
        "With $C=H\\mathsf SH=\\mu I+\\nu X$, each edge change of the complex network is a product of kernels $C_v^{\\pm1}$ over a residual basis, a $C$-layer on $e=mf$ axes needs exactly $s$ child layers on $f$ axes, and the normalized butterfly layer is $H_0^{\\otimes D}=\\nu^D\\mathcal S_DC^{\\otimes D}\\mathcal S_D$.",
        "Compute the algebra of $C$ and $H_0$, conjugate a residual basis into standard coordinate directions by a binary change of basis built from row additions, and use $C^{-1}=-iZCZ$ so that inverse kernels also need one forward child.",
        "The network can only supply kernels in coordinate directions, but a residual basis is arbitrary, so it must first be moved to coordinate positions by an invertible binary change of basis that need not preserve the dot product.",
        "recoverable",
        page="phase-frame-kernel",
        frm=["common-frame-principle", "subset-intersection-labels", "rank-saving-recurrence", "butterfly", "linear-algebra-basics", "complex-numbers"],
        adds=[("f", "slot width", "The number of axis chunks in one of the $m$ consecutive slots into which a group of $e=mf$ chunks is divided.", "count")],
        salient="The same two-point kernel $C$, whose square is the bit flip $X$ and whose fourth power is the identity, serves every residual direction, and inverse kernels need no extra child call.",
        children=[
            infer("The kernel is $C=H\\mathsf SH=\\mu I+\\nu X$ with $\\mu=(1+i)/2$ and $\\nu=(1-i)/2$.",
                  r"C=H\mathsf SH=aI+bX,\qquad a=\frac{1+i}{2},\quad b=\frac{1-i}{2}."),
            infer("Direct multiplication gives $H_0=\\nu\\mathsf SC\\mathsf S$, $C^2=X$, $C^4=I$, $C^{-1}=-iZCZ$.",
                  r"H_0=b\mathsf SC\mathsf S,\qquad C^2=X,\qquad C^4=I,\qquad"),
            infer("An edge from label $U_-$ to $U_+$ gives the frame change $\\prod_{j}(\\mu I+\\nu X_{v_j})^{\\epsilon_j}$ over an orthonormal basis $v_1,\\dots,v_{\\rho_{\\rm edge}}$ of its residual.",
                  r"=\prod_{j=1}^{\rho_{\rm edge}}(aI+bX_{v_j})^{\epsilon_j},"),
            infer("Extending the residual basis to an invertible binary $M$ and conjugating, $\\mathcal P_MX_v\\mathcal P_M^{-1}=X_{Mv}$, turns the basis directions into standard coordinate directions.",
                  r"\mathcal P_MX_v\mathcal P_M^{-1}=X_{Mv}."),
            infer("A group of $e=mf$ consecutive axis chunks is divided into $m$ slots of $f$ chunks, and column $j$ collects the selected bit from chunk $j$ of every slot.",
                  r"For the array layout used below, take a group of $e=mf$ consecutive"),
            infer("For each $h\\le\\rho_{\\rm edge}$, applying $(C^{\\epsilon_h})^{\\otimes f}$ to the $f$ bits of slot $h$ is one child operation on $f$ axes.",
                  r"This is one child operation"),
            infer("A negative kernel still needs one forward child: $(C^{-1})^{\\otimes f}=(-i)^fZ^{\\otimes f}C^{\\otimes f}Z^{\\otimes f}$, with wrappers that are only address signs and fourth-root phases.",
                  r"=(-i)^f Z^{\otimes f}C^{\otimes f}Z^{\otimes f}."),
            infer("Gaussian elimination writes the binary change of basis as at most $m(m-1)+3m$ elementary row additions, a fixed number, each applying $x_{h,j}\\leftarrow x_{h,j}\\oplus x_{h',j}$ for all $0\\le j<f$.",
                  r"as a product of at most $m(m-1)+3m$ elementary row additions: one clears"),
            infer("Summing residual dimensions gives exactly $s$ child calls, each on the $f=e/m$ axes of one slot.",
                  r"child calls, each on the $f=e/m$ axes of one slot."),
            infer("Applying the identities on all selected coordinates gives $H_0^{\\otimes D}=\\nu^D\\mathcal S_DC^{\\otimes D}\\mathcal S_D$ with $(\\mathcal S_Dz)(x)=i^{x_1+\\cdots+x_D}z(x)$.",
                  r"H_0^{\otimes D}=b^D\mathcal S_D C^{\otimes D}\mathcal S_D."),
        ])


def PSB():
    return show(
        "A binary row addition $y_{j_i}\\leftarrow y_{j_i}\\oplus x_{j_i}$ on the same bit position of $f$ chunks at once, with a third chunk restored to its arbitrary contents, costs $O(V(L^{\\tau}+1))$ plus a small repair term, where $L=fK$ is the width of the three chunks.",
        "Apply eight cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges that act, away from a small set of exceptional addresses, as independent segment additions and together flip the parity of the target bit, then repair the exceptional set by sorting.",
        "Each XOR on a selected bit is a carry-free addition mod 2 only if the segments cannot carry into each other, so segments get guard bits and the few addresses where a guard is near its edge are handled separately.",
        "explicit",
        mq=r"of cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges, then corrects an explicitly bounded set of",
        page="packed-selected-bit-addition",
        frm=["controlled-shift", "address-chunk-interchange", "elementary-stream-ops", "binary-representation-and-bit-operations", "modular-arithmetic"],
        salient="Eight conditional additions to two integers $u,w$ flip the parity of $u$ and restore $w$ for every initial $w$: the cycle $(00\\ 01\\ 10)$ followed by $(01\\ 10\\ 11)$ composes to $(00\\ 10)(01\\ 11)$.",
        adds=[("L", "chunk width", "The width $fK$ in bits of each of the three chunks $x,y,z$ involved.", "count"),
              ("K", "segment width", "The number of bits $K\\ge6$ in each segment of a chunk.", "count")],
        children=[
            infer("The time is $O(V(L^\\tau+1)+MA_{\\rm rect}^3+\\delta_{\\rm bad}MA_{\\rm rect}(R+A_{\\rm rect}))$ with $\\delta_{\\rm bad}=\\min\\{1,80(f-1)2^{-K}\\}$.",
                  r"O\!\left(V(L^\tau+1)+MA_{\rm rect}^3+"),
            infer("Eight operations $u\\leftarrow u\\mp c[w\\equiv0],\\ w\\leftarrow w\\mp c[u\\equiv0],\\dots$ are performed in the displayed chronological order, each with a Boolean control $c$.",
                  r"u&\leftarrow&u-c[\,w\equiv0\pmod2\,],&(1)"),
            infer("For $c=1$ the first four operations induce the cycle $(00\\ 01\\ 10)$ on the parities $(u,w)$ in every square $\\{2a,2a+1\\}\\times\\{2b,2b+1\\}$.",
                  r"induce the cycle $(00\ 01\ 10)$ in every square"),
            infer("The last four induce $(01\\ 10\\ 11)$, so the two cycles compose to $(00\\ 10)(01\\ 11)$.",
                  r"$(00\ 10)(01\ 11)$."),
            infer("The complete output is $(u',w')=(u+c(1-2(u\\bmod2)),w)$: the parity of $u$ flips exactly when $c=1$ and the auxiliary $w$ is restored.",
                  r"\bigl(u+c(1-2(u\bmod2)),\ w\bigr)."),
            infer("Omit the top selected position temporarily; the other $f-1$ segments of $K$ bits are disjoint, with guard values $g_i,h_i$ and controls $c_i=x_{j_i}$.",
                  r"Omit the most significant selected position $j_{f-1}$ temporarily. For"),
            infer("Each line is a single cyclic shift of the target chunk by an offset determined by the two control chunks, so each line is a bijection of the rectangle.",
                  r"Physically, a line updating $y$ by sign $\varepsilon$"),
            infer("An address is bad when some used guard value fails $10\\le g\\le2^{K-1}-11$; elsewhere no segment carries into or borrows from the next, even with displacement eight.",
                  r"10\le g\le 2^{K-1}-11."),
            infer("There are exactly twenty excluded values per guard, so the union bound over $2(f-1)$ guards gives $|\\mathcal B|\\le\\delta_{\\rm bad}M$.",
                  r"|\mathcal B|\le\delta_{\rm bad}M."),
            infer("The ideal map $T_0$ and the packed map $S_0$ are bijections that agree off $\\mathcal B$, so $T_0S_0^{-1}$ fixes $\\mathcal B^c$ and permutes $\\mathcal B$.",
                  r"Thus $T_0S_0^{-1}$ fixes $\mathcal B^c$ and permutes $\mathcal B$."),
            infer("The rotations use at most sixteen chunk swaps and eight rotations in $S_0$, each rotation an $O(V)$ controlled shift.",
                  r"There are at most sixteen swaps and eight rotations in $S_0$."),
            infer("Exceptional records are extracted with their destination ranks and sorted by stable binary radix sort on the keys, which are distinct because $T_0S_0^{-1}$ permutes $\\mathcal B$, then reinserted in one scan.",
                  r"while stable binary radix sorting scans only the extracted records and"),
            infer("The omitted top selected position is handled by a two-bit XOR of elementary-stream cost $O(V)$.",
                  r"Finally apply the two-bit XOR to $x_{j_{f-1}},y_{j_{f-1}}$ by"),
            infer("Under $A_{\\rm rect}=O(p)$, $R$ above every fixed polynomial of $p$, $f\\le d\\le p$ and $K/\\log p\\to\\infty$, the extra terms vanish and the cost is $O(V(L^\\tau+1))$; a whole change of basis on a stream costs $O(V((eK)^\\tau+1))$.",
                  r"the complete change of basis and its inverse therefore cost"),
        ])


def FBL():
    guard = show(
        "All intermediate coefficients of the layer are represented exactly with $p+\\Delta$ fractional bits and $\\Delta+3$ integer and sign bits per real component, where $\\Delta=\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil=o(p)$.",
        "Bound the number of additions, halvings and sign changes on any chain from an input coefficient by a recurrence in the depth of the recursion, then convert chain length into denominator and magnitude growth.",
        "Intermediate values of the network are not contractions, so exactness requires reserving enough bits for the longest chain of dyadic operations; chain length multiplies by the branching $s$ at each level.",
        "recoverable",
        adds=[("\\Delta", "guard width", "The number $\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil$ of extra fractional bits reserved, with $C_1=20$ and $C_0=128mB^2$, $B=s+E$, $E=64(W+m+1)^3$.", "count")],
        children=[
            infer("Set $E=64(W+m+1)^3$ and $B=s+E$, with $\\nu=\\min\\{j:m^j\\ge B\\}$, $C_1=\\max\\{20,\\nu+3\\}$, $C_0=128mB^2$ and $\\Delta=\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil$.",
                  r"E=64(W+m+1)^3,\qquad B=s+E,"),
            infer("The longest operation chain obeys $A(e)\\le sA(e/m)+E$ at an internal node and $A(e)\\le8e$ at a leaf; the depth concatenates all $s$ calls, so it uses $s$ rather than $s/W$.",
                  r"A(e)\le sA(e/m)+E"),
            infer("Unrolling over at most $\\lceil\\log_md\\rceil$ levels with $sx+E\\le Bx$ gives $A(e)\\le8dB^{k+1}\\le8B^2d^{\\nu+1}$.",
                  r"A(e)\le 8dB^{k+1}\le8B^2d^{\nu+1}."),
            infer("Adding the piece concatenation, outer diagonals and scalar $b^D$ gives a total below $128mB^2d^{\\nu+3}\\le C_0d^{C_1}$ for both denominator and magnitude growth.",
                  r"<128mB^2d^{\nu+3}\le C_0d^{C_1}."),
            infer("A value at depth $\\ell$ lies in $2^{-(p+\\ell)}\\mathbb Z[i]$ with modulus at most $2^\\ell$, so $p+\\Delta$ fractional bits and $\\Delta+3$ integer bits suffice and shifts discard no bits.",
                  r"lies in \(2^{-(p+\ell)}\mathbb Z[i]\) and has modulus at most"),
            infer("Choosing $\\epsilon C_1<1$ with $d=\\Theta(p^\\epsilon)$ makes $\\Delta=o(p)$, so the full coefficient width stays $O(p)$.",
                  r"Choose \(\epsilon C_1<1\)."),
        ])
    rows = show(
        "The rows needed to feed the network come from the first $q_0$ chunks, each recursive call then runs on a stream of exactly $1/W$ of its parent's volume, and per-node overheads are polynomial in $p$ and absorbed by the length of the polynomial record.",
        "Apply individual $C$ kernels on the first $q_0=\\lceil\\log_2W\\rceil\\lceil\\log_m(2d)\\rceil$ chunks, treat their bits as a row index, pad rows to a multiple of $W^{k_0}$, and deal rows to the $W$ roles at every node.",
        "The network needs rows divisible by $W^{k_0}$ to deal them evenly at each of the $k_0$ levels; individual kernels on a few chunks supply these rows at cost $O(V\\log d)$.",
        "recoverable",
        children=[
            infer("One individual $C$ kernel is a split at the selected bit, a paired arithmetic scan and a merge, so $e$ individual kernels cost $O(Ve)$.",
                  r"Thus \(e\) individual kernels cost"),
            infer("At an internal node, binary basis changes, scalar gates, endpoint corrections and reassembly cost $O(V((eK)^\\tau+1))$, with $m$ slots of $(e/m)K$ address bits each.",
                  r"O\bigl(V((eK)^\tau+1)\bigr)"),
            infer("Put $q_0=\\lceil\\log_2W\\rceil\\lceil\\log_m(2d)\\rceil$ and $k_0=\\lceil\\log_md\\rceil$; if $D\\le q_0$ use individual kernels at cost $O(V\\log d)$.",
                  r"q_0=\lceil\log_2 W\rceil\,"),
            infer("Treat the concatenated bits of the first $q_0$ chunks as one row index of range at least $W^{k_0}$.",
                  r"regard their concatenated bits as one row index."),
            infer("Zero rows are added to reach the next multiple of $W^{k_0}$, growing the volume by a factor at most two.",
                  r"the same number of zero rows to reach the next multiple of \(W^{k_0}\);"),
            infer("A row $u=Wg+w$ is sent entirely to wire role $w$, so each recursive edge call has volume exactly $1/W$ of its parent's.",
                  r"and send that entire row to physical wire role \(w\), indexed there"),
            infer("The $W$ role streams use a fixed number of tapes: other streams are pushed on a LIFO tape before a child call and popped after it.",
                  r"child, push the other role streams onto a LIFO work tape, copy the"),
            infer("Each padded zero row is again zero when an invocation finishes, so added rows can be deleted at the end.",
                  r"Each padded zero row is again zero when an invocation finishes, so the"),
        ])
    return show(
        "The layer $\\mathcal H=\\bigotimes H_0$ acting on the same bit of $D\\le d$ address chunks of polynomial records is applied exactly, then truncated once, in $O(Vd^{\\lambda'})$ time with error less than $\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}$.",
        "Evaluate the layer exactly in Gaussian dyadics through the phase-frame network (a second instance of the rank saving), implement the binary basis changes by packed chunk moves, convert $C^{\\otimes D}$ to $H_0^{\\otimes D}$ by diagonal phases, and truncate once.",
        "Applying $D$ separate butterfly factors would need $D$ whole-array passes; the network replaces $e$ simultaneous kernels by $s$ kernels on $e/m$ axes, with call-volume ratio $s/W<m$, saving a power of $D$.",
        "explicit",
        mq=r"factors would require $D$ whole-array passes.",
        page="fast-butterfly-layer",
        frm=["phase-frame-kernel", "packed-selected-bit-addition", "rank-saving-recurrence", "address-chunk-interchange", "fixed-point-truncation", "polynomial-ring-mod", "butterfly"],
        salient="The layer is evaluated exactly, with no intermediate rounding, even though the intermediate operators of the network are not contractions; all rounding is one truncation after the contractive layer is complete.",
        adds=[("D", "selected axes", "The number $1\\le D\\le d$ of consecutive $K$-bit chunks whose bit at a common offset takes part in the layer.", "count")],
        children=[
            PFK(),
            PSB(),
            rows,
            infer("Writing $D-q_0=\\sum_ja_jm^j$ in base $m$ and treating the pieces separately, the total is $O(\\log(2d)[d^\\lambda+d^{\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)}+1])=O(d^{\\lambda'})$ for any $\\max\\{\\lambda,\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)\\}<\\lambda'<1$.",
                  r"Write \(D-q_0=\sum_j a_jm^j\) in base \(m\), with \(0\le a_j<m\)."),
            guard,
            infer("Proposition: the procedure returns $Q_p(\\mathcal Hz)$ in the same array and format in $O(Vd^{\\lambda'})$ time, with all returned coefficients in the unit disk.",
                  r"The procedure returns $Q_p(\mathcal H z)$ in the same array,"),
            infer("Every row of $\\mathcal H$ has absolute row sum one, so $\\mathcal H$ maps disk arrays to disk arrays, and componentwise truncation preserves the disk with error below $\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}$.",
                  r"Finally, every row of $\mathcal H$ has"),
        ])
